    "title": "Iowa DNR holds informational meeting on 12-mile Lake restoration - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Travis Chinn ,\n1 days ago\nWALLINGFORD , Iowa ( KCAU ) — Wednesday night in Wallingford, IA, the Iowa Department of Natural Resources (DNR) held an informational meeting on the restoration of 12-Mile Lake.\n12-Mile Lake is a 220-acre lake that sits just west of Wallingford in Emmet County.\nThe lake is popular with hunters, kayakers, and bird watchers.\nStory continues below\nThe DNR wants to do a partial \"draw-down\" of the lake, or drain part of it to help encourage vegetation growth and improve water quality.\n\"Right now, if you go and look at 12-Mile Lake, it's pretty turbid/green, brown-type water. When we get done, what we hope to see is clear water. Water you can actually go down and see the bottom. But with that, you’ll see emergent vegetation and submergent vegetation,\" said Bryan Hellyer, NW District Wildlife Supervisor for the Iowa DNR.\nThe estimated cost of the project is $180,000.\nThe DNR hopes to start the project in 2027 and hopefully have it completed in 2028 or 2029.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed.\nFor the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KCAU 9 News | Sioux City, IA | News, Weather, and Sports.\n2",
    "summary": "WALLINGFORD , Iowa ( KCAU ) — Wednesday night in Wallingford, IA, the Iowa Department of Natural Resources (DNR) held an informational meeting on the restoration of 12-Mile Lake. The lake is popular with hunters, kayakers, and bird watchers. The DNR wants to do a partial \"draw-down\" of the lake, or drain part of it to help encourage vegetation growth and improve water quality. But with that, you’ll see emergent vegetation and submergent vegetation,\" said Bryan Hellyer, NW District Wildlife Supervisor for the Iowa DNR. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed.",
